{"archetypes":[{"name":"cleanup-tmp-descendant-protection","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The old cleanup.sh used the same `should_remove_by_age` (with depth-5 descendant scan) for both cache and /tmp entries; the new code adds `cache_entry_has_fresh_descendant` only for cache entries — /tmp larch pattern dirs are now removed based on top-level mtime alone even if they have fresh descendants from an active session.","prompt_body":"Examine `skills/cleanup/scripts/cleanup.sh` in the diff. The old code used `should_remove_by_age` with `newest_activity_mtime` (depth-5 scan) uniformly for both `~/.cache/larch/sessions/` and `/tmp` larch pattern entries. The new code adds `cache_entry_has_fresh_descendant` protection for cache entries but applies a bare `find -mtime +N` without any descendant check for `/tmp` entries. Determine whether a long-running `/implement` session whose `/tmp/claude-implement-*` top-level dir has a stale mtime but active deep descendants would now be prematurely deleted. Check whether this asymmetry is documented consistently across SECURITY.md, cleanup.md, and the test plan, and whether any test covers the `/tmp` descendant-fresh case. Also look for the naming inconsistency in test-cleanup.sh where the work directory is named `stale-toplevel-with-fresh-deep-child-removed` but the assertions and test-cleanup.md description say the directory should be kept (`CACHE_REMOVED=0`). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"fixup-commit-scope-and-scoping","focus_area":"edge-cases","weight":1,"rationale":"The new ship_pr_pre_rebase_larch_logs_fixup function in ship-pr.sh stages and commits only `larch-logs/` but both the outer guard check and the second-pass re-check are also scoped to `larch-logs/` — if a pre-commit hook modifies tracked files outside that subtree, neither pass closes the Guard-1 window.","prompt_body":"Review `scripts/ship-pr.sh` around the new `ship_pr_pre_rebase_larch_logs_fixup` function (the `0b` block in `run_rebase_rebump`). The function runs `git add -u -- larch-logs/` and the outer guard is `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no -- larch-logs/`. If a pre-commit hook triggered by `git-commit.sh` writes to tracked paths outside `larch-logs/`, the second-pass re-check and the function's re-staging are both still scoped to `larch-logs/`, leaving those files dirty for Guard 1. Check whether the test cases in `scripts/test-ship-pr.sh` (`rebump_dirty_tracked_fixup_idempotent_hook`) fully cover this scenario or only cover hooks that write to `larch-logs/`. Also check whether `fail_file` is declared `local` inside the helper, since the outer `run_rebase_rebump` also uses `fail_file` and the helper reassigns it without a `local` guard. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"find-mtime-boundary-semantics","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"The refactor replaces epoch-second arithmetic (stat-based CUTOFF) with `find -mtime +N`, and introduces a `find ... | grep -q .` pattern in `cache_entry_has_fresh_descendant` whose return-value contract and BSD/GNU portability across macOS and Linux CI both deserve scrutiny.","prompt_body":"Look at `skills/cleanup/scripts/cleanup.sh` in the diff. The old code computed `CUTOFF=$((NOW - RETENTION_DAYS * 86400))` and compared `newest < CUTOFF` using exact epoch seconds; the new code uses `find -mtime +\"$RETENTION_DAYS\"`. Verify that `find -mtime +N` semantics (strictly more than N×24 hours ago, truncated) are equivalent to the old epoch arithmetic across both BSD find (macOS) and GNU find (Linux CI), specifically around the boundary case where mtime is within the same 24-hour bucket as the cutoff. Separately, review `cache_entry_has_fresh_descendant`: it uses `find … -print -quit 2>/dev/null | grep -q .`; confirm whether a zero-output `find` (no fresh descendants) causes `grep -q .` to exit non-zero and whether that correctly makes the function return non-zero (so the caller proceeds with deletion). Also confirm that a `find` error (permission denied on a deep subdir) would pipe an empty stream to `grep -q .`, causing silent skip rather than an `larch_err` warning; compare against the old code which emitted an explicit warning and skipped deletion. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"test-scenario-coverage","focus_area":"testing","weight":1,"rationale":"Four targeted depth-based preservation tests (depth1, depth2, depth4, depth5) plus date-failure-errors and find-failure-skips-deletion were removed; the plan's stated test coverage for the awk harness includes expected-failure has_key probes under set -e which require careful set+e/set-e brackets.","prompt_body":"Compare the removed test cases in `skills/cleanup/scripts/test-cleanup.sh` against what is now present. The diff removes `stale-with-fresh-depth1-child`, `stale-with-fresh-depth2-grandchild`, `stale-with-fresh-depth4-manifest`, `stale-with-fresh-depth5-round`, `date-failure-errors`, and `find-failure-skips-deletion`. Determine which failure modes those tests covered that no longer have any coverage: the `date +%s` failure path no longer exists in the new code (so that removal is correct), but find-failure behavior has changed from per-entry warnings to per-pass warnings — verify that the new pass-level warning path (`larch_err \"Warning: find age-pass failed...\"`) is tested. Also inspect `scripts/test-design-structure.sh` around lines 517–530: the new `grep -Fq -- '--snapshot-trailers'` and `grep -Fq -- '--dedup'` pins use `--` to prevent BSD grep from interpreting the leading dashes as options — verify this is applied correctly on all four new grep lines and that the surrounding `|| fail` labels name the correct file and anchor. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
